  "subject": "🪄 Axios Finish Line: The smile effect",
  "date": "Wed, 28 Oct 2026 21:29:20 -0400",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n 🚲 Plus: Kindness in the cold | Wednesday, October 28, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Axios Finish Line \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n By Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei &middot; Oct 28, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n Good Wednesday evening! Smart Brevity™ count: 411 words … 1½ mins. Copy edited by Amy Stern. \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 1 big thing: Don&#x27;t wait for a reason to smile \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Your smile could be a self-fulfilling prophecy.\n\n Why it matters: Simply smiling when you're stressed or blue can lift your spirits, research shows — even when you don't totally feel it. 🔎 Zoom in: Several studies have found evidence that the physical act of smiling can boost feelings of happiness.\n\n People who smile while performing stressful tasks tend to have lower heart rates than those with neutral expressions, Judy Ho, a Los Angeles clinical and forensic neuropsychologist, told AP . Smiling while running can help you relax and run more efficiently, Outside Magazine reports . The intrigue: The findings support the idea that physical actions and sensations can help shape emotions, rather than simply reflect them, Stanford researchers write .\n\n A smile, even a forced one, can make us feel happier. A furrowed brow can spark feelings of anger. 🎒 Case in point: At the Orange County Academy of Sciences and Arts in Southern California, the power of a smile is taken very seriously. An entire section on the charter school's website is dedicated to its philosophy that a smile can make a difference.\n\n \"Smiling creates a supportive classroom culture that makes students relaxed, active, and motivated,\" according to the website. \"It increases emotional well-being, lowers stress, and gives students confidence.\" Smiling isn't a magic fix. One analysis of 138 studies found that the emotional benefits of facial expressions are modest and vary depending on the circumstances.\n\n But it's a free, instant tool to keep in your back pocket next time you're feeling down. Go deeper . \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA MESSAGE FROM AXIOS\r\n\n \r\n See what's next for media in 2027 \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Media Trends Executive Annual members receive the 2026 Annual Report, our special media document featuring global forecasts, sector analysis and exclusive media data insights.\r\n\n\r\n \r\n🔒 Get the report by becoming a Media Trends Executive member.\r\n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 2. 🧤 Kindness du jour \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n A random act of wintertime kindness, shared by reader Joan Orman of Galesburg, Mich.:\n\n \"The temps were hovering around 20°, and it was past dark when I stopped off at a Kmart store on my way home from work. I was on my bicycle, a little over 4 miles from home. As I exited the store, I realized that I didn't have my big puffy gloves with me. Remembering that I'd set them on a bench to lock up my bike, I was quite dismayed to see that they were no longer there.\" \"This would not be a good ride home. As I got closer to my bike, I saw that some kind passerby had noticed the gloves, balled them up and stuck them in the water bottle cage on my bike. That angel saved my digits!\" \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 🏁 Please invite your friends to join Finish Line . \n\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
